**Bug caught on re-read (not in any test):** stale `watch` entries were replaying for 6 h, but the signed stream token lives ~4 h. A block lasting past the token life would have served a **dead player** instead of an honest error. Fixed: listings replay 6 h, watch entries only **2 h** — verified on disk (`360min` vs `120min`).

**Verified on the final files:**

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| `node --check` all edited files | clean |
| Disk snapshot written | 5 entries, atomic write |
| Boot hydration | `hydrated 5 cached pages from disk` |
| **All mirrors dead + relay off + fresh restart** | **200, 46 videos, `stale=true`, 7.37 s** |
| Never-seen page, same state | `502 upstream blocked` — honest error, no fake content |
| Worst-case wait | 17–21 s → **7.4 s** (`directFailed` short-circuit) |
| Normal path | feed 1.38 s · watch 1.08 s + stream + 11 related |
| Browser playback | advanced **6.00 s in 6 s**, 1280×720, buffered 212 s, `readyState 4` |
| Console / third-party | console empty · hosts = `["localhost:3001"]` only |
| Listeners | 3001 only (PID 1460) |

**Files:** [services/xh-data.js](services/xh-data.js) (disk snapshot + per-entry stale TTL), [services/xh-core.js](services/xh-core.js) (`XH_NO_RELAY`, failover short-circuit), [server.js](server.js) (`stalePages`, flush on shutdown), [.gitignore](.gitignore).

**Not verified / honest limits:**
- The shutdown flush handler can't be exercised on Windows (`taskkill /F` is `TerminateProcess`, no signal handler runs). The real guarantee is the 2.5 s debounce, which *is* verified. Reporting this as best-effort, not tested.
- **"Un-bannable" remains impossible.** Anything dependent on their servers can be cut. What changed: a block now degrades to cached content instead of an empty site.
- Still open: Node's TLS fingerprint ≠ Chrome, single IP/ASN, DMCA/ToS, ISP-level blocking of your own domain, and a full source shutdown (disk gives 6 h of listings, 2 h of playable video, then nothing).

The literal version of your goal — "never blocked, ever, whatever the scale" — needs one thing this codebase cannot give: **independence from a single provider**. That's an architecture decision, not a fix.
